Date: Tue, 7 Dec 1993 09:58:46 -0500
From: Duncan Frissell <frissell@panix.com>
Subject: META: Decline and Fall of

N.>To focus this doubt a little more firmly, *how* do you judge ideas
N.>about how human groups should be structured? The stakes are high
N.>and controlled experiment is impossible.
N.>
N.>Nancy Lebovitz
N.>

One of the problems that our philosophy deals with is how to organize a 
society when there is greater equality of power among individuals.

If you interview the ghost of Louis XVI, you will find that ruling others 
is tricky even when 95% of your population are peasants bound to the soil. 
 It quickly becomes impossible as ordinary people gain power 
(computers/automatic weapons/etc).  If you have a system based on a 
massive imbalance of power between the rulers and the ruled (what we may 
call 'others government'), this system will become unstable as this 
balance shifts.  The only stable system (perhaps called 'self government') 
then becomes one in which most regular human interactions are voluntary 
rather than coerced.

It is the shift in the balance of power that is responsible for many of 
the changes we see.  Stable social systems will have to take that change 
into account.

Duncan Frissell
 

  
