> 
> Actually, it also resembles charge cards, debit cards and ATM cards more 
> than it does cash.  Names are important--especially in marketing.  I have
> some suggestions:
> 
> ( Secure- | Safe- | Priva- ) ( Checks | Charge )

Names are important, primarily as they relate to the position you are 
trying to establish in your prospect's mind. The problem with DigiCash
or whatever you call it is that I've never seen anyone give a good
argument as to how to position this (this topic came up in the VirtSem
on startups recently). 

What problem are you solving that would lead someone to want this product?
Ability to make purchases remotely? I do that regularly with my traditional
credit and debit cards. Anonymity of transactions? While I might see
the value in this, most people associate a desire for anonymous transactions
with seediness (using cash to pay a prostitute or illegal immigrant). This
association might kill the product for most prospects. Security of 
transactions? I can get that with cash or charge cards today.

It seems that the problem that DigiCash(tm) solves (or rather, the
benefit that it provides) is:

anonymous, secure, transactions over computer networks

Unfortunately, that position is much to complex to make it in the
market.

Now, if you want to offer a product called "The Privacy Card", you
could probably get it to succeed.

