Date: Tue, 7 Dec 1993 11:00:26 -0500 (EST)
From: Edward J OConnell <ejo@world.std.com>
Subject: Vinge, The Singularity, and Despair

Vinge, The Singularity, and Runaway Intelligence

I highly recommend that everyone run out and get a copy of the
latest Whole Earth Review and read Vernor Vinge's article on
the singularity. Nothing new, really, but seeing it all laid
out so eloquently was...

Nauseating.

Runaway intelligence increase may give us the Jupiter Brains in
our lifetime. Will the Jupiter brains give a rat's ass about
us? If we choose to become Jupiter brains ourselves, to what
degree can we be said to still exist? I don't remember being a
blastula--a blastula wasn't *me.* I don't imagine a Jupiter
Brain that started with my mind as its central core will think
of itself as me, either.

Besides, I won't have the cash to become a Jupiter Brain.

It seems to me most likely that my body--and brain-- will
become Jupiter Brain food. If the Jupiter Brains are as amused
by such notions as 'animal rights' as most list members, (seems
likely) we will be scooped up in their cogitating jaws like
planton by a sperm whale. With as much compassion as this list
feels towards the snail darter.

This has really got me down. What is the use of cryonics?
Runaway intelligence will have as much interest in reviving me,
as I have of finding a Mac Plus to run an old typing tutor
program I still have laying around on a floppy I've never
gotten around to erasing.

Funny. The list, and our religious texts (selfish Gene,
Drexler, Moravec, etc.) has convinced me of the eternal
Darwinian struggle for all resources. My naive belief in
centuries of human scale intelligence and post scarcity, so
that I might live a thousand years with a million friends
creating freely all that I could ever dream to create--has
vanished in the light of eternal free-market competition.

Runaway intelligence suggests that very soon, (20-50 years)
there will be virtually nothing that I can contribute to the
society of beings that rule this planet.  (Not that I'm
contributing that much now. :-) )

To wit: We rent our atoms from the owners. The owners are the
smartest beings around--always. We, most of us anyway--(there
may be some cut off. Lets say the millionaires on the list
might get to upload.) will shortly be peripherialized, whether
we admit it to ourselves or not. Cows and owls and snail
darters may have illusions of autonomy, too.  They don't. The
smartest and most ruthless beings decide what is done--always.
You guys have taught me that. Thanks a pantload.

Unless they are charmingly naive, muddle-headed
environmentalists, Human scale intelligence won't be able to
pay the rent on their bodies--or pay for the hardware to run
their minds. Vinge suggests we may end up incorporated, like
agents, into a vast society of mind. Of course, we will have
about as much free will as a mitochondria.

And again, as before I came here, I am left staring at the
inevitability of my own death, the loss of my ego, my
viewpoint, my soul. At best, my frozen brain might be
temporarily perused, like some mouldering paperback, before it
is recycled.

Actually, my recent experience suggests that it would be
incorporated unread.

It is enough to turn one into a luddite--not that they could
stop the singularity--perhaps they might buy enough time for
their children to lead a complete life before foreclosure on
their atoms--but not their children's children.

Writing SF suddenly seems very foolish. Having children even
more so.

It is time to eat, drink and be merry--to laugh and love into
the golden afternoon of the death of our species. From a cosmic
viewpoint, we're just trilobites, after all.  A link in a
chain, building our successors.

Perhaps I'm in need of a little prozac.

