It appears highly likely that such a phenomenon is prevalent. I was
fortunate in that both of my parents gave me physical closeness and
reassurance. School, for me, was one long string of abuse from other
males for being a "wuss". Most of my friends were (and still are)
female; I have recently hypothesized that this is due to my view of
women as more tolerant, more open-minded, more...fair, as fnerd says.
 
To Tim, Nancy writes:
 
>Do you know that Paglia's a lesbian? You still might enjoy her in-your-
>face attitude, but she isn't displaying herself for men.
 
Even if she were exclusively lesbian, it wouldn't make any difference to
me. But let me see if that copy of Vanity Fair is still within arm's
reach... here it is, September 1992, page 302.
 
[begin quote]
 
Like Madonna, Camille Paglia wishes to be a glamorous gender bender,
dishing with gay men, dallying with her own sex. BORN-AGAIN BISEXUAL,
says a button on her desk. The problem is getting the other team to
play. Political correctness is in danger of making gay men a bunch of
pills, complains Paglia. The anger of ACT UP is unleavened by any sense
of joyful ministry. Gay men at their pagan-studliest celebrate play,
physicality, pretense, not to mention Greco-Roman grappling. For all the
flak she has gotten in the gay press (an item in QW accused her of
skipping a gay friend's funeral because she feared her star presence
would be a distraction, and took what Paglia interpreted as a sideswipe
at Lauren Hutton), she affirms that the two biggest influences on her
are the Catholic Church and gay men. With gay men she could appreciate
glamour unabashedly. Lesbians lack that aesthetic feel. They're trumps,
sleeping bags with legs.
 
"I conclude that to my regret exclusive lesbianism is emotional retardation
[...] When lesbians cut themselves off from men, the end result [is]
that the middle-aged lesbian personality is childish. It's infantile,
okay? I'm not saying stop being lesbians. I'm not saying stop sleeping
with other women. You must. But you must accept your power over men --
you cannot be constantly trapped in this ideology of male power, male
