Nancy also writes:
 
>I've noticed various sorts of prejudice against men--not just
>the destroyers of the earth variety, but also the assumption
>that men being killed or injured isn't as important as
>women or children being hurt.
 
Just thinking about this one brings to mind a few dozen reasons as to
the WHY of this one. It still makes me cringe. I got into my share of
fights with girls at school as well, and it was infuriating to see, on a
daily basis, the incredible license and leeway these girls were given to
set up their own little feudal kingdoms. They could do no wrong; they
were never questioned or bullied; only the boys, it seemed, were
dangerous and in need of constant control.
 
Luckily, I had enough female friends outside of school to avoid
stereotyping them. But I've never been under the illusion that the
asshole contingent is restricted to either gender, or any race, for that
matter -- for example, I've always held the definition of "nigger", in
my mind, to be a lying bastard who rips you off, kicks you in the balls
and spits on you before running away at top speed. And there's niggers
of every color and sex in the world, and probably in the universe, too.
 
>...women and children may be more protected (though I'd
>like to see some numbers on people who die from wars), but
>if it's necessary to risk your neck to get status, then
>being protected means having low status.
 
I'll buy that.
 
 
fnerd@smds.com (Future Nerd) writes extensively on the idea of Guys
operating on a sense of "fairness", a sort of internal "justice system",
or "due process game". I question whether this is (or should be) an
exclusively male approach. Every female I have known in my life that I
have liked/respected/admired/loved/whatever -- that I have positively
appreciated -- has dealt with me on these terms. I consider it the
essence of free market relationships, that is, peaceful interaction.
 
"If you don't play fair, you're an asshole, and I don't wanna play with
you. Leave me alone. If you don't leave me alone, if you fuck with me,
I'll hurt you back. I'm not afraid to hurt you back, because if the
grownups find out, it's your fault, because you started it."
 
Every kid I've ever tried this on understands it. They know the
difference between the initiation of force and the use of force in
self-defense. And they know the difference between free will and
coercion (cf. James Donald's essay on Natural Law and Rights).
 
Dr. Sandy Bem's "Bem Sex Role Inventory" is suggested as further reading
on the subject of gender. It was posted some time ago to the LIBFEM
mailing list. If there is sufficient interest, I will repost it here.
 
I contend that if children are raised properly -- with love, fairness
and respect -- and their gender treated as secondary to their worth as
an individual, that they will have a higher degree of self-esteem and
mental/emotional health; i.e., that they will be better equipped to
approach life as something to flourish in for as long as possible, not
merely survive for a short time and then mercifully leave behind for the
unproven hope of something better.
 
(Are you happy, fnerd? I *did* quote you. :)
 
 


